        <mxCell id="panelD_note" value="TRAINED (v4) spreads trips MORE evenly across these same 42 drivers than real dispatch did (lower spread, no single driver above 74 trips vs. REAL's single busiest driver at 166) -- an emergent property of the model's own optimization, not a fairness rule anyone added. Still doesn't use all 42 (4 idle) -- this backtest runs with zero exploration (always picks the single best driver, never rotates); more training runs and/or a small amount of exploration in evaluation should spread usage further." style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#FFF2CC;strokeColor=#D6B656;fontSize=10;align=left;verticalAlign=middle;spacingLeft=12;spacingRight=12;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="60" y="1534" width="1380" height="65" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>

        <!-- ===== Bottom: honest interpretation banner ===== -->
        <mxCell id="interp_hdr" value="Honest Interpretation" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#FFF2CC;strokeColor=#D6B656;fontSize=14;fontStyle=1;align=center;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="60" y="1620" width="1380" height="35" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="interp_body" value="100% missed-opportunity recovery is NOT proof of an exceptional model -- real order volume in this window was genuinely light (~13.7 orders/driver across ~5.5 real months), so real fleet-wide capacity likely existed the whole time; not a claim the real dispatcher made 130 provable mistakes (real-world constraints like contract terms and driver preference aren't visible in this data). The cycle-based results above are the more rigorous test: every one of REAL's own 770 real driver-cycles is counted, none excluded -- a figure and its supporting metrics that only became fair and complete after THREE direct user corrections (documents/logs/27 follow-ups, real bugs each time, not reassurances): REAL judged by the same idle-timeout standard as TRAINED, empty miles priced for the WHOLE cycle not just the closing leg, and TRAINED's own cycle reconstruction no longer silently drops the 130 recovered orders it's credited for everywhere else in this dashboard (plus a real RNG-determinism bug fixed along the way -- these numbers are now byte-identical across reruns, verified directly). With all fixes in, TRAINED now wins or ties on every operational metric here (distance-to-home, deadhead miles per cycle, missed-opportunity recovery, work distribution, HOS margin preserved) except cycle-level trip/revenue density -- down purely because TRAINED earns MORE total revenue spread across EVEN MORE, shorter cycles, a direct, disclosed consequence of prioritizing driver-home-time, confirmed independently by the paired-comparison test's real revenue cost (p=0.0188, see documents/logs/27). This model (v4) IS now the deployed live model -- this dashboard is not a pre-deployment gate, it's the honest record of the tradeoff that was accepted." style="text;html=1;whiteSpace=wrap;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=middle;fontColor=#5C4A00;spacingLeft=15;spacingRight=15;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="60" y="1660" width="1380" height="95" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>

        <mxCell id="footer" value="Source: sim/backtest/real_data_replay.py --cycles (cycle-based panels) and --restrict-drivers (missed-opportunity/work-distribution panels), run against state_value_function_v4_hours_since_home.pkl (documents/logs/27). A prior &quot;HOS Daily Utilization&quot; panel is not reproduced here -- the analysis that computed it is no longer part of real_data_replay.py; removed rather than shown with stale numbers." style="text;html=1;whiteSpace=wrap;fontSize=10;fontStyle=2;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;fontColor=#999999;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="60" y="1770" width="1380" height="40" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>

      </root>
    </mxGraphModel>
  </diagram>
